WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.700
When Caliban first steps onto the screen in
Logan, it's impossible to tell who's under

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.000
that killer costume.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.930
But after he took off his bandana and goggles,
a little bell of recognition probably went

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:13.980
off in your head — there's something familiar
about the photosensitive mutant tracker.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:17.859
Caliban is played by British writer, comedian,
and actor Stephen Merchant.

00:00:17.859 --> 00:00:21.660
But he's had quite a career long before he
landed the choice role of Charles Xavier's

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:22.660
caretaker.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.490
So where on Earth have we seen this guy before?

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:29.390
Merchant began his career doing stand-up and
sketch comedy before meeting Ricky Gervais

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:33.870
in 1997, when Gervais gave him a job at London's
X-F-M radio station.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:38.160
The duo hosted a cult weekend radio show for
several years, introducing the world to their

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.440
future podcast co-host Karl Pilkington …

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.440
“You do realize…”

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.140
"Sorry, I've gotta stop you, mate, cuz the
little earpiece has come off in his ear!"

00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:51.960
… and later teamed up as co-creators of
one of the most critically acclaimed comedies

00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:53.969
of all time: The Office.

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:58.390
In addition to creating the comedy classic,
Merchant also had a small appearance in series

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:01.789
two, showing up as one of Gareth's friends,
nicknamed the "Ogg Monster" …

00:01:01.789 --> 00:01:05.199
"Don't have a go at the eyes, because that
is astigmatism that I've had from the age

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:07.970
of five, so that's what makes them a bit bulbous."

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:11.820
Merchant and Gervais both served as executive
producers on the American version of the show.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.530
and wrote the episode "The Convict," which
means Merchant helped give the world "Prison

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:16.530
Mike"...

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.820
"What was the food like in prison?"

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.590
"Gruel… sandwiches.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:22.590
Gruel omelettes.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.219
Nothin’ but gruel."

00:01:24.219 --> 00:01:26.670
… and for that, the world is eternally grateful.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:31.740
In 2005, Merchant and Gervais followed-up
The Office with another acclaimed cringe comedy,

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:32.740
Extras.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:35.820
The series follows Gervais as a desperate
background actor trying to break into the

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:36.820
spotlight.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:38.040
Merchant plays his incompetent agent ...

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.990
"It took me two days to realize they're eight
hours ahead."

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.159
"They're behind."

00:01:41.159 --> 00:01:42.159
"Are they?"

00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:43.159
"Yes."

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:43.880
"That explains quite a lot."

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.860
Merchant's performance scored him a best actor
prize at the British Comedy Awards.

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:53.009
That same year, Merchant and Gervais launched
the popular Ricky Gervais Show podcast, which

00:01:53.009 --> 00:01:55.420
was later reimagined as a cartoon for HBO
…

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:56.780
"Even cavemen had little pants on.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:57.780
Why haven't they—"
"Whoa!"

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:01.180
"Slow down."

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:02.180
"Rewind."

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:07.220
In 2011, the two reunited for Life's Too Short,
a series based around the fictionalized life

00:02:07.229 --> 00:02:11.480
of Warwick Davis, the actor who played Wicket
the Ewok in Return of the Jedi.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:16.360
And in 2010, Merchant and Gervais produced
and appeared in An Idiot Abroad, a documentary

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:21.830
series featuring Karl Pilkington being sent
to exotic locations …often against his will.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:26.860
In 2007, Merchant made his big screen debut
in Edgar Wright's Hot Fuzz alongside Simon

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:32.120
Pegg…and appeared very briefly again that
same year with Pegg in Run Fatboy Run.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.400
In 2009, Merchant had a quick cameo in the
Ricky Gervais-helmed The Invention of Lying,

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.110
but his profile got much higher the following
year, when Merchant showed up alongside Dwayne

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:44.280
Johnson in Disney's Tooth Fairy, playing The
Rock's otherworldly case worker.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.370
Since then, Merchant appeared briefly in Cemetery
Junction, which he also wrote and directed

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:52.420
with Gervais, played third fiddle to Jason
Sudeikis and Owen Wilson in the Farrelly brothers

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:57.470
flop Hall Pass, and shared the screen with
Halle Berry in the dreadful Movie 43, among

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:58.980
a smattering of other small roles.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.560
And of course, if you didn't catch any of
those movies, you may have run across Merchant

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:03.750
while holding a controller.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:08.000
Video game fans could never forget his role
as the sweet-then-sadistic Wheatley from Portal

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.000
2:

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.440
"I honestly do wish I could take it all back,
and not just cuz I’m stranded in space.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.940
I know who you are, mate!"

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.620
Merchant finally struck out on his own in
2013 with HBO's Hello Ladies.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:23.470
The short-lived series followed the character
of Stuart Prichard, an Englishman who's moved

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:26.690
to Los Angeles in the hopes of becoming a
big-time ladies man.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:29.780
Of course, Stuart isn't as debonair as he
thinks.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:33.239
Instead, he's the kind of socially awkward
jerk who lies about a handicapped friend being

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:36.459
a war vet so he can worm his way inside a
swanky club.

00:03:36.459 --> 00:03:39.769
Merchant filled the show with moments from
his actual love life, using his "most pitiful

00:03:39.769 --> 00:03:42.269
dating stories" to create some truly awkward
scenes …

00:03:42.269 --> 00:03:44.459
"Wade, as in Roe v. Wade.

00:03:44.459 --> 00:03:46.620
It was a famous case about a woman's right—
"

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:47.620
"They know what it is.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:51.519
No need to bring up abortion — til we need
to."

00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:55.550
Hello Ladies only lasted one season before
HBO pulled the plug, although Merchant was

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:58.930
able to give viewers closure with a story-ending
movie.

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:02.370
Merchant has appeared on Comedy Central's
Drunk History three times, always playing

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:03.370
an American president.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:06.610
He's donned a top hat twice to play Abraham
Lincoln, as well as sporting a wig for the

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:07.610
part of George Washington.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:11.530
"The guy doesn't even speak English, so he's
says blah blah blah blah blah blah blah."

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:15.110
It only makes sense that Merchant has played
two of America's taller presidents.

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:19.520
But even though Washington stood at 6'2" and
Lincoln measured up at 6'4", the 6-foot-7-inch

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:20.581
Englishman still has them beat.

00:04:20.581 --> 00:04:24.440
In 2015, Merchant helped create the show Lip
Sync Battle.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:28.660
It all started in 2013, when Merchant was
talking with The Office's John Krasinski about

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:32.410
what Krasinski should do for his upcoming
appearance on Late Night with Jimmy Fallon,

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:34.760
and they decided on a lip syncing contest.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:38.510
Fallon reused the skit multiple times, with
Merchant once coming aboard to face off against

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:39.580
Fallon and Joseph Gordon-Levitt.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:43.630
Soon, the concept made its way over to Spike,
where it became Lip Sync Battle.

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:47.920
The lanky Merchant even once donned a crop
top and pair of chaps for his take on Christina

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.360
Aguilera's "Dirty" …

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.800
“That’s when we take it to the parking
lot, and I bet you somebody’s gonna call

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:53.780
the cops.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:54.780
Uh-oh, here we go.”

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.639
If you still don't recognize Merchant, maybe
his appearance on the most popular sitcom

00:05:01.639 --> 00:05:02.980
in America will jog your memory?

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:08.080
In 2015, he appeared as math teacher Dave
Gibbs on the CBS smash Big Bang Theory, dating

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.320
Amy following her break-up with Sheldon.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:14.320
Dave turns out to be a Sheldon superfan, asking
fanboy questions about his favorite theoretical

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.480
physicist rather than actually having a real
conversation with his date.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:21.480
But all's well that ends well, since Dave
helps Sheldon and Amy get back together, encouraging

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.350
the socially awkward scientist to patch things
up …

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:27.200
"Kiss her, you brilliant fool!”

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.080
Thanks for watching!

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:31.850
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:34.280
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

